;; clojerl2 known divergences from JVM Clojure (designs/04 §5.1, D12;
;; finalized in clojerl2-025). User-facing version: docs/differences.md.
;;
;; Every deliberate difference is registered here with
;;   :reason       what clojerl2 does and why
;;   :design       the design section that decided it
;;   :alternative  what to write instead
;; and is checked two ways:
;;   * evidence: clj2_divergence_evidence_tests has one test per id
;;     (`:a/b-c' → a__b_c_test) showing the behavior; it fails if the
;;     divergence goes away, and the ids and tests must match one for one;
;;   * the corpus gate (`make certify'): a failing corpus entry may cite an
;;     id here as its cause in test/corpus-known-failures.edn.
;; clj2_divergences_tests asserts both, and that docs/differences.md
;; covers every id.
[{:id :lazy-seq/per-process-realization
  :area :lazy
  :reason "Lazy seqs and delays memoize in the process dictionary: another process walking the same lazy seq realizes it again. Values agree for pure thunks; a side-effecting thunk runs once per process. The ets memo switch (clj2_lazy:set_memo/1) realizes once across processes."
  :design "designs/03 §5.3"
  :alternative "Keep side effects out of lazy seqs (use doseq/run! for effects), or realize the seq (doall, vec) before handing it to another process."}
 {:id :lazy-seq/bounded-memo
  :area :lazy
  :reason "The memo store is bounded (clj2_lazy:memo_limit/0, default 100000 cells); past it the store is cleared, and a lazy seq walked again afterwards realizes again. Keeps long-lived processes from growing without bound."
  :design "designs/03 §6"
  :alternative "Realize a seq you walk many times into a vector; raise the limit with clj2_lazy:set_memo_limit/1 if a process legitimately holds more."}
 {:id :lazy-seq/no-chunking
  :area :lazy
  :reason "No chunked seqs: realization is one element at a time (map over a vector does not realize 32 at once). An optimisation, not semantics."
  :design "designs/03 §6"
  :alternative "Nothing to change; code that relied on chunks realizing ahead (side effects in map) should not."}
 {:id :symbol/no-metadata
  :area :reader
  :reason "Symbols are atoms and carry no metadata of their own: (meta '^:foo bar) is nil and with-meta on a symbol fails. Metadata written on a def name is kept, also through the defining macros ((defn ^:private f ...), (defmulti ^:dynamic m ...)): the compiler carries the reader's metadata on a macro call's name over to the def of that name in the expansion."
  :design "designs/03 §1.1"
  :alternative "Keep metadata on vars and collections, not on symbol values; a macro that needs a name's metadata should take it as an explicit map argument."}
 {:id :char/integer
  :area :strings
  :reason "A character is its integer code point; there is no separate char type: \\a is 97, (str \\a) is \"97\", (seq \"ab\") is (97 98), (= \\a 97) is true, char? is true of any integer that is a code point, and format's %c takes one. Corpus rows that test char rendering are overridden in test/corpus-overrides.edn (project decision 2026-10-12: keep code points)."
  :design "designs/03 §1.1, §7"
  :alternative "Build strings from chars with (apply str (map (fn [c] (unicode/characters_to_binary (list c))) cs)) or work on strings (subs, clojure string fns) rather than chars."}
 {:id :string/code-point-indexing
  :area :strings
  :reason "Strings are UTF-8 binaries indexed by code point: count, nth and subs count code points, so (count \"😀\") is 1 (JVM: 2 UTF-16 units), and nth/subs walk the binary (O(n), JVM O(1))."
  :design "designs/03 §7"
  :alternative "Traverse with seq/reduce rather than indexing in a loop; convert once with (unicode/characters_to_list s) when you need random access."}
 {:id :number/no-ratio-arithmetic
  :area :numbers
  :reason "(/ 1 2) is 0.5: integer division that does not divide exactly gives a float; ratios read but are not computed with."
  :design "designs/03 §1.1"
  :alternative "Use quot/rem for exact integer division; keep numerator and denominator yourself where exact fractions matter."}
 {:id :number/beam-semantics
  :area :numbers
  :reason "Arithmetic follows BEAM, not the JVM (a project decision): integers are unbounded (no 64-bit long overflow, wrap-around, truncation or range checks: parse-long of a big number is that number, ^long/^double hints coerce nothing, bit ops take bignums, %x of a negative number is signed); floats compare exactly, so 0.0 and -0.0 differ; float division by zero raises badarith and ##Inf/##NaN raise an ArithmeticException when evaluated (BEAM floats have no Inf/NaN; they read, so a file that mentions one still compiles); the casts byte, short, int, long, char and float do not range-check ((byte 128) is 128)."
  :design "designs/03 §1.1; user decision 2026-10-11 (clojerl2-023)"
  :alternative "Check argument types yourself where a JVM program relied on ClassCastException; mask with (bit-and x 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) where 64-bit wrap-around was intended; test for zero before dividing floats."}
 {:id :fn/arity-at-compile-time
  :area :compiler
  :reason "A direct call to a fn var with an arity it does not have is a compile error (JVM: ArityException at run time), because vars link directly to module functions."
  :design "designs/03 §2.1a"
  :alternative "Call through a value when the arity is only known at run time: (apply f args), or a local (let [g f] (g 1 2)), raises at run time as on the JVM."}
 {:id :vars/forward-references
  :area :compiler
  :reason "A namespace file (or anything compiled as one unit) declares all its top-level vars before compiling any form, so a fn body may call a var defined further down the file (JVM: Unable to resolve symbol, a compile error). At the REPL, where each form is its own unit, an undefined name is still a compile error."
  :design "designs/03 §2.1 (clj2_p_declare: forward references without fake_fun)"
  :alternative "Nothing to change for code that compiles on the JVM; use declare where the JVM needs it, for code meant to run on both."}
 {:id :macros/defmacro-form
  :area :compiler
  :reason "defmacro is a form the compiler handles itself, not a macro: (macroexpand-1 '(defmacro m [a] a)) returns the form unchanged (JVM: a do with a defn taking &form and &env and a .setMacro call)."
  :design "designs/01 §5 (parse: kernel forms)"
  :alternative "Do not rely on the expansion of defmacro; use (:macro (meta #'m)) to ask whether a var is a macro."}
 {:id :interop/erlang-module-calls
  :area :compiler
  :reason "A call head ns/f whose qualifier is neither an alias nor a known namespace names an Erlang module ((lists/reverse x) calls lists:reverse/1), so an unknown alias is not a compile error: the call fails when it runs (undef). JVM: 'No such namespace' when the form compiles."
  :design "designs/09 §2 (resolve: the remaining mod/fun call heads are erl_call), §3 (Erlang calls)"
  :alternative "Require a namespace before the forms that use its alias (a require inside a fn body aliases nothing at compile time on the JVM either); a misspelt alias shows up as undef when the call runs."}
 {:id :fn/structural-identity
  :area :compiler
  :reason "Fns are Erlang funs, which compare by value: two closures of the same fn form over equal captured values are = and identical?, and collapse as set members or map keys (JVM: every evaluation of a fn form is a distinct object)."
  :design "designs/03 §1.1, §2.1"
  :alternative "Do not use fn identity as an identity token; tag values explicitly (a map with an id) instead."}
 {:id :exceptions/erlang-classes
  :area :compiler
  :reason "catch selects by JVM class through clj2_class's model of the JVM hierarchy, but the errors underneath are Erlang's: an Erlang error is given the nearest JVM class (badarith is ArithmeticException, a function_clause or badarg IllegalArgumentException, an index out of range IndexOutOfBoundsException, anything else RuntimeException), the runtime's own errors carry their JVM class in ex-data :type, and Erlang exits and throws are caught only by :exit, :throw, :default, Exception or Throwable. A JVM class the model does not know is a compile error. Dereferencing a future whose body threw rethrows that exception itself (JVM: wrapped in an ExecutionException)."
  :design "designs/09 §3; clojerl2-021 follow-up (catch by class, 2026-10-12)"
  :alternative "Catch the class you mean as on the JVM; where the exact JVM subclass of an Erlang error matters, catch a broader class (RuntimeException) and test (ex-data e) or the message in the handler."}
 {:id :protocols/type-designators
  :area :protocols
  :reason "extend-type takes JVM classes and interfaces only as far as clj2's class model knows them (java.lang, java.util and clojure.lang: Object, String, Number, Map, IPersistentVector ...), dispatching through the value's class ancestry; clj2 type tags (integer, vector, map ... default) work too. There is no reify, and classes outside the model (java.io.File, user Java classes) cannot be extended."
  :design "designs/03 §4.1a"
  :alternative "Use a defrecord plus extend-type where reify was used; extend a class the model knows (or a clj2 tag) instead of one it does not."}
 {:id :namespaces/hot-code-two-versions
  :area :namespaces
  :reason "Redefinition follows BEAM's two-version code loading: reloading a namespace, or redefining a var at the REPL (each REPL var has a module of its own), keeps the previous version alive; fn values taken from it keep running the old code while new calls run the new one. A second redefinition purges the oldest version: fn values still holding it raise badfun when called, and a process still executing it makes the redefinition fail (old_code_in_use). A var given a value at run time instead (a def inside an expression, alter-var-root, with-redefs) moves it into the var table once and is reassigned there from then on: no more versions, and every caller sees the new value."
  :design "designs/02 §5, designs/03 §2.2"
  :alternative "Hold the var's name rather than its value when you want the latest definition: call (f ...) directly or through a wrapper fn (fn [& args] (apply f args))."}
 {:id :vars/core-direct-linked
  :area :namespaces
  :reason "clojure.core is linked directly into everything compiled against it, itself included, so the root of a clojure.core fn or constant var cannot change at run time: alter-var-root, with-redefs and a def inside an expression of such a var raise IllegalStateException naming this id. (JVM 1.8+: core is direct-linked too, but user code calls core through the var, so with-redefs of a core fn reaches user code.) Dynamic core vars (*out*, *print-length* ...) are bound and set as usual."
  :design "designs/03 §2.1a"
  :alternative "Redefine your own var that wraps the core fn, and call that: (defn my-deref [x] (deref x)), then (with-redefs [my-deref ...] ...)."}
 {:id :vars/self-calls-direct
  :area :namespaces
  :reason "Once a var of a namespace module is given a value at run time (with-redefs, alter-var-root, a def inside an expression), calls between the module's vars go through the var table, but a fn's calls of itself stay inside its own body, like recur: (alter-var-root #'f (fn [old] (fn [n] (if (zero? n) :new (old n))))) never reaches the new fn from old's recursion (JVM: the recursive call goes through the var)."
  :design "designs/03 §2.1a, §2.2"
  :alternative "Make the recursion go through a value you control: have the fn take itself as an argument, or recur on a helper the new value can wrap."}
 {:id :namespaces/symbols
  :area :namespaces
  :reason "A namespace is its symbol: *ns*, in-ns, the-ns, find-ns and create-ns deal in symbols (there are no Namespace objects), and *ns* prints as the bare name."
  :design "designs/03 §2.1"
  :alternative "Use (ns-name *ns*) or *ns* where a name is needed; code that called Namespace methods needs the clj2 fns instead."}
 {:id :dynamic/per-process-bindings
  :area :namespaces
  :reason "Dynamic bindings live in the process dictionary. future, agent actions and bound-fn carry the caller's bindings along, as on the JVM; any other process (erlang/spawn, an Erlang callback) starts with none and sees the roots. The current namespace and *1/*2/*3/*e at the REPL are bindings too."
  :design "designs/03 §3"
  :alternative "Wrap a fn handed to erlang/spawn or to Erlang code in bound-fn, or pass the values explicitly as arguments or in messages."}
 {:id :refs/no-stm
  :area :state
  :reason "Refs and STM are not provided: ref, dosync, sync, alter, commute, ref-set and ensure raise an ex-info saying so. BEAM has no multi-object transactional memory, and a fake one would lie about isolation."
  :design "designs/03 §5.2"
  :alternative "Keep state that changes together in one value: one atom holding a map (swap! updates it atomically), or one agent / gen_server that owns it and is updated by message."} {:id :state/explicit-lifetime
  :area :state
  :reason "Mutable cells are not reclaimed when they become garbage (BEAM has no finalizers or weak references): an atom that has been written keeps its ETS row, a promise or future its result row, and an agent its process, until the VM stops. An atom that is only read costs nothing. A volatile lives in the process dictionary of the process that made it, until that process exits, and cannot be read from another process."
  :design "designs/03 §5.2"
  :alternative "Create long-lived atoms and agents once (def them) rather than per call in a long-running process; keep per-request scratch state in local bindings, loop/recur state or the process's own state, and give short-lived work its own process."}
 {:id :agents/process-per-agent
  :area :state
  :reason "Each agent is a process that runs its actions in arrival order. send and send-off share that one queue (there are no thread pools); a send made inside an action is dispatched at once rather than held until the action succeeds; shutdown-agents does nothing."
  :design "designs/03 §5.2"
  :alternative "Nothing to change for ordinary use; do not rely on sends from a failing action being dropped, nor on shutdown-agents to stop agents."} {:id :values/no-identity
  :area :state
  :reason "BEAM values have no object identity: identical? is exact equality (=:=), so two equal vectors, maps or large numbers are identical?, and compare-and-set! swaps when the current value equals the expected one (JVM: when it is the same object)."
  :design "designs/03 §1.1, §5.2"
  :alternative "Do not use identical? or compare-and-set! to tell apart equal values; carry an explicit version or id in the value when you need to (e.g. {:version n ...})."} {:id :transients/wrapped
  :area :collections
  :reason "A transient wraps the persistent value (BEAM terms are immutable): conj!, assoc! and the other bang ops return the next transient, so code that uses their return values, as Clojure requires, works; a transient used again after persistent!, or an old transient used after a bang op, is not detected and simply holds the value it held. Transients cost what persistent updates cost."
  :design "designs/03 §1.1"
  :alternative "Use the return value of every bang op (as Clojure demands anyway); there is no speed to gain from transients here, so plain persistent ops are equally fine."} {:id :collections/map-entry-vectors
  :area :collections
  :reason "A map entry is a two-element vector; there is no separate MapEntry type. So map-entry? is true of any two-element vector, key and val take one, and empty of an entry is [] (JVM: map-entry? is false of a vector, key/val of a vector throw, empty of a MapEntry is nil)."
  :design "designs/03 §1.1"
  :alternative "Do not use map-entry? to tell entries from pairs you built; key and val work on both."}
 {:id :sorted/comparator-calls
  :area :collections
  :reason "Sorted maps and sets are balanced trees of their own, not the JVM's red-black PersistentTreeMap: building or updating one calls the comparator a different number of times (fewer, typically). Order, lookup and equality are the same."
  :design "designs/03 §1.3"
  :alternative "Keep comparators free of side effects; do not count their calls."}
 {:id :collections/exact-keys
  :area :collections
  :reason "Hash maps and sets are Erlang maps keyed by the exact term: two keys that are = but differ in representation (a list and a vector with the same elements) or in metadata are different keys, so (get {[1 2] :a} '(1 2)) is nil and assoc of such a key adds an entry instead of replacing the stored one. Keys that are numbers, strings, keywords, symbols or collections of one kind behave as on the JVM."
  :design "designs/03 §1.3"
  :alternative "Use one kind of collection for composite keys (vectors, say) and do not rely on metadata on map keys or set members; (vec k) normalizes a key that may arrive as a seq."} {:id :collections/map-order
  :area :collections
  :reason "Maps iterate in Erlang's internal map order (fixed for a given set of keys, independent of how the map was built), not insertion order: on the JVM an array-map (any map of up to 8 entries, or one built with array-map) keeps insertion order, so keys, vals, seq, printing and everything built on them (frequencies, group-by, merge, zipmap ...) may list entries in a different order. Equality, lookup and hashing are unaffected."
  :design "designs/03 §1.3"
  :alternative "Do not rely on map order (Clojure does not promise it for hash maps either): sort the entries, keep an ordered vector of keys beside the map, or use a sorted-map when order matters."} {:id :host/jvm-classes
  :area :interop
  :reason "There is no JVM: interop syntax reaches host shims (clj2_jvm) that implement the commonly used members of java.lang, java.util and clojure.lang (Integer/parseInt, Math, String methods, exception constructors, StringBuilder, Var/Symbol/Keyword ...). Other classes are not available: java.io files, streams, readers and URLs, java.net, java.text and java.time/Calendar (and so format's %t), seeded java.util.Random, named charsets, Thread objects. Using one raises IllegalArgumentException (no matching method)."
  :design "designs/09 §3"
  :alternative "Use the Clojure functions (slurp, spit, clojure.string, format without %t, rand) or the Erlang modules directly: file, filename, io, calendar, unicode, rand, erlang:system_time ((file/read_file path), (calendar/universal_time) ...)."} {:id :reader/platform-feature
  :area :interop
  :reason "The reader-conditional platform feature is :clje (as in clojerl 1.0), not :clj: #?(:clj a :clje b) reads b, and a #? with only :clj and :cljs branches reads as nothing. A .cljc library's :clj branches are JVM code (Java classes, interop), so they are skipped rather than compiled here. (read-string {:read-cond :allow :features #{:clj}} s) adds a feature when it is wanted."
  :design "designs/01 §1.1"
  :alternative "Give BEAM code a :clje branch (or a :default one); read-string's :features option adds features for data written for another platform."} {:id :seqs/class-model
  :area :collections
  :reason "Seqs are Erlang lists (finite, realized) or lazy seqs: class reports clojure.lang.PersistentList or clojure.lang.LazySeq where the JVM has a family of seq classes (Cons, LongRange, Range, Iterate, Repeat, Cycle, ChunkedCons, ArraySeq, APersistentMap$KeySeq ...), so (class (range 5)), instance? tests against those classes and printing of their class names differ. Seq behavior (first, rest, count, =, hash) is the same."
  :design "designs/03 §6"
  :alternative "Test with the predicates (seq?, sequential?, coll?, counted?) rather than class or instance? on concrete seq classes."}]
